{
  "summary": "Checks for pending peer orphan transactions under m_tx_download_mutex before acquiring cs_main in ProcessOrphanTx. When a peer has no orphan transactions to reconsider, ProcessMessages returns early without locking cs_main. A test is added verifying that ProcessMessages completes without cs_main contention when no orphan work is pending.",
  "problem": "ProcessMessages calls ProcessOrphanTx for each connected peer on every loop pass (at least every 100 ms), acquiring cs_main unconditionally even when the peer has no orphan work. This causes the P2P message handler to unnecessarily block on validation, RPC, or wallet threads holding cs_main, delaying processing of unrelated messages such as pings.",
  "evidence": [
    "Author benchmark: when another thread holds cs_main for 50 ms, handling a ping message in ProcessMessages drops from 50.1 ms on master to 0.02 ms with this change (PR description)."
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [],
    "enables": [
      "#36244"
    ]
  },
  "scope_notes": "Small change (+38/-0 across 2 files) including a unit or functional test; the diff was omitted from the prompt.",
  "changed_since_previous": "",
  "card": "Skips acquiring cs_main in ProcessOrphanTx when a peer has no orphan transactions queued, checking under m_tx_download_mutex first. Currently, ProcessMessages takes cs_main unconditionally for every peer on every message-handling pass, causing message processing to block on active validation or RPC operations. In author benchmarks with cs_main held for 50 ms, ping handling latency dropped from 50.1 ms to 0.02 ms. Intended to reduce lock contention independently while paving the way for asynchronous block processing in #36244.",
